WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.795
Aesop’s Fables. Part 12.

00:00:02.795 --> 00:00:04.606
The Farmer and Fortune.

00:00:04.606 --> 00:00:10.784
A Farmer was ploughing one day on his farm when he turned up a pot of golden coins with his plough.

00:00:10.784 --> 00:00:18.492
He was overjoyed at his discovery, and from that time forth made an offering daily at the shrine of the Goddess of the Earth.

00:00:18.492 --> 00:00:27.594
Fortune was displeased at this, and came to him and said, "My man, why do you give Earth the credit for the gift which I bestowed upon you?

00:00:27.594 --> 00:00:38.351
You never thought of thanking me for your good luck; but should you be unlucky enough to lose what you have gained I know very well that I, Fortune, should then come in for all the blame."

00:00:38.351 --> 00:00:41.189
Show gratitude where gratitude is due.

00:00:41.189 --> 00:00:42.868
Jupiter and the Monkey.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:52.668
Jupiter issued a proclamation to all the beasts, and offered a prize to the one who, in his judgment, produced the most beautiful offspring.

00:00:52.668 --> 00:01:00.692
Among the rest came the Monkey, carrying a baby monkey in her arms, a hairless, flat-nosed little fright.

00:01:00.692 --> 00:01:16.749
When they saw it, the gods all burst into peal on peal of laughter; but the Monkey hugged her little one to her, and said, "Jupiter may give the prize to whomsoever he likes: but I shall always think my baby the most beautiful of them all."

00:01:16.749 --> 00:01:18.258
Father and Sons.

00:01:18.258 --> 00:01:27.854
A certain man had several Sons who were always quarrelling with one another, and, try as he might, he could not get them to live together in harmony.

00:01:27.854 --> 00:01:32.504
So he determined to convince them of their folly by the following means.

00:01:32.504 --> 00:01:38.699
Bidding them fetch a bundle of sticks, he invited each in turn to break it across his knee.

00:01:38.699 --> 00:01:48.301
All tried and all failed: and then he undid the bundle, and handed them the sticks one by one, when they had no difficulty at all in breaking them.

00:01:48.301 --> 00:01:59.920
"There, my boys," said he, "united you will be more than a match for your enemies: but if you quarrel and separate, your weakness will put you at the mercy of those who attack you."

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:01.482
Union is strength.

00:02:01.482 --> 00:02:02.430
The Lamp.

00:02:02.430 --> 00:02:12.730
A Lamp, well filled with oil, burned with a clear and steady light, and began to swell with pride and boast that it shone more brightly than the sun himself.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:15.837
Just then a puff of wind came and blew it out.

00:02:15.837 --> 00:02:22.979
Some one struck a match and lit it again, and said, "You just keep alight, and never mind the sun.

00:02:22.979 --> 00:02:28.143
Why, even the stars never need to be relit as you had to be just now."

00:02:28.143 --> 00:02:29.978
The Owl and the Birds.

00:02:29.978 --> 00:02:42.343
The Owl is a very wise bird; and once, long ago, when the first oak sprouted in the forest, she called all the other Birds together and said to them, "You see this tiny tree?

00:02:42.343 --> 00:02:54.445
If you take my advice, you will destroy it now when it is small: for when it grows big, the mistletoe will appear upon it, from which birdlime will be prepared for your destruction."

00:02:54.445 --> 00:03:06.202
Again, when the first flax was sown, she said to them, "Go and eat up that seed, for it is the seed of the flax, out of which men will one day make nets to catch you."

00:03:06.202 --> 00:03:16.118
Once more, when she saw the first archer, she warned the Birds that he was their deadly enemy, who would wing his arrows with their own feathers and shoot them.

00:03:16.118 --> 00:03:23.434
But they took no notice of what she said: in fact, they thought she was rather mad, and laughed at her.

00:03:23.434 --> 00:03:32.145
When, however, everything turned out as she had foretold, they changed their minds and conceived a great respect for her wisdom.

00:03:32.145 --> 00:03:39.479
Hence, whenever she appears, the Birds attend upon her in the hope of hearing something that may be for their good.

00:03:39.479 --> 00:03:46.691
She, however, gives them advice no longer, but sits moping and pondering on the folly of her kind.
